{"id":"how-to-revise","title":"How to revise so it sticks","subject":"Study skills","label":"Study skills","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":166,"duration":165.56,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":441,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"The puzzle","start":0,"end":13.885},{"id":"explain","title":"Why and how","start":13.885,"end":79.725},{"id":"example","title":"Worked session","start":79.725,"end":99.235},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":99.235,"end":112.605},{"id":"answer","title":"The answer","start":112.605,"end":121.565},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common slips","start":121.565,"end":142.655},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":142.655,"end":165.56}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":5.45,"text":"You close the book feeling confident, then the exam paper opens and nothing comes.","w":[0.52,0.7,1.06,1.18,1.44,1.8,2.9,3.12,3.3,3.72,4.12,4.58,4.7,5]},{"s":0,"a":5.75,"b":13.46,"text":"That gap between familiar and known is the puzzle we will solve. I'm Kora, and today I'll show you how to revise so it sticks.","w":[5.82,6.08,6.38,6.8,7.4,7.52,7.98,8.1,8.26,8.62,8.74,8.92,9.92,10.12,10.92,11.04,11.4,11.6,11.8,11.94,12.1,12.2,12.68,12.86,12.96]},{"s":1,"a":14.31,"b":23.59,"text":"A large review by John Dunlosky and colleagues found something useful. Rereading and highlighting help very little. Testing yourself and spacing your study help most.","w":[14.38,14.48,14.88,15.24,15.48,15.78,16.46,16.6,17.12,17.36,17.76,18.78,19.36,19.5,20.06,20.3,20.58,21.3,21.68,22.12,22.26,22.68,22.84,23.16,23.4]},{"s":1,"a":23.89,"b":29.9,"text":"Rereading fools you because familiar feels like knowing. You recognise the page, but recognition is not recall.","w":[23.96,24.54,24.84,25.16,25.44,25.86,26.12,26.28,26.76,26.96,27.46,27.58,28.16,28.36,29.06,29.22,29.48]},{"s":1,"a":30.2,"b":35.47,"text":"Testing makes your brain work to pull ideas out. That struggle is what strengthens memory.","w":[30.22,30.62,30.84,31.02,31.34,31.6,31.72,31.98,32.42,33.36,33.66,34.18,34.28,34.5,35.06]},{"s":1,"a":35.77,"b":43.05,"text":"Spacing means leaving a gap before you return to the topic. Interleaving means mixing topics or question types in one session.","w":[35.86,36.36,36.64,36.96,37.06,37.38,37.68,37.82,38.16,38.26,38.36,39.36,39.94,40.28,40.68,41.22,41.4,41.84,42.24,42.46,42.62]},{"s":1,"a":43.35,"b":50.96,"text":"So here are five habits. One: test yourself before you feel ready. Close the notes, write what you remember, then check.","w":[43.42,43.6,43.8,43.94,44.22,45.36,45.9,46.16,46.6,46.9,47.04,47.26,47.98,48.3,48.42,49.1,49.3,49.44,49.56,50.38,50.62]},{"s":1,"a":51.26,"b":57.81,"text":"Two: space it out instead of cramming. Come back the next day, then after three or four days, then a week.","w":[51.3,51.82,52.18,52.32,52.58,52.9,53,53.86,54.1,54.36,54.48,54.7,55.54,55.78,56.06,56.3,56.42,56.6,57.34,57.54,57.6]},{"s":1,"a":58.11,"b":65.38,"text":"Three: mix your topics. Mixed practice feels harder, but it teaches you to recognise which method a question needs.","w":[58.14,58.98,59.22,59.36,60.44,60.76,61.18,61.5,62.3,62.46,62.56,62.9,63.04,63.18,63.82,64.12,64.44,64.52,64.94]},{"s":1,"a":65.68,"b":71.51,"text":"Four: explain it in your own words, as if teaching a younger student. Notice where you get stuck.","w":[65.76,66.42,66.88,67.02,67.1,67.34,67.54,68.38,68.52,68.66,69,69.1,69.42,70.4,70.7,70.86,70.98,71.16]},{"s":1,"a":71.81,"b":79.3,"text":"Five: practise under exam conditions with past or practice questions. Time yourself, then mark and learn from the mistakes.","w":[71.88,72.62,73.14,73.38,73.76,74.22,74.42,74.82,74.94,75.36,76.32,76.6,77.54,77.82,78.18,78.32,78.54,78.7,78.78]},{"s":2,"a":80.15,"b":86.71,"text":"Try a twenty-five minute session on one topic. First, spend five minutes recalling from memory with the notes closed.","w":[80.15,80.37,80.48,81.15,81.54,81.98,82.15,82.37,82.76,83.15,83.49,83.76,84.21,84.76,85.04,85.43,85.71,85.93,86.27]},{"s":2,"a":87.01,"b":93.04,"text":"Next, spend ten minutes on practice questions. Then spend five minutes checking your mistakes and fixing them.","w":[87.12,87.8,88.08,88.3,88.72,88.84,89.24,90.28,90.52,90.86,91.14,91.44,91.74,91.88,92.4,92.54,92.86]},{"s":2,"a":93.34,"b":98.81,"text":"Finally, spend five minutes writing questions for next time. That gives your next session a clear start.","w":[93.44,93.92,94.28,94.62,94.92,95.2,95.66,95.8,96.02,96.9,97.12,97.32,97.52,97.74,98.16,98.24,98.54]},{"s":3,"a":99.66,"b":102.98,"text":"Now a quick check. You have one hour for Biology tonight.","w":[99.74,99.98,100.06,100.3,101.06,101.22,101.56,101.72,102,102.16,102.68]},{"s":3,"a":103.28,"b":109.71,"text":"Which is better: rereading the chapter twice, or closing the book and answering questions on it, and why?","w":[103.32,103.56,103.7,104.56,105.02,105.18,105.58,106.38,106.54,106.94,107.04,107.36,107.56,107.92,108.42,108.54,109.12,109.28]},{"s":3,"a":110.16,"b":112.18,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[110.18,110.5,110.72,110.86,111.14,111.68,111.86]},{"s":4,"a":113.03,"b":121.14,"text":"Close the book and answer questions. That is better because testing makes you retrieve ideas and shows gaps, while rereading only feels familiar.","w":[113.08,113.44,113.56,114.04,114.28,114.56,115.64,115.86,116,116.32,116.64,116.98,117.18,117.32,117.76,118.16,118.28,118.58,119.46,119.7,120.26,120.48,120.72]},{"s":5,"a":121.99,"b":129.21,"text":"One mistake is rereading and highlighting and calling that revision. Avoid it by closing the notes and writing what you remember first.","w":[122.14,122.28,122.76,122.92,123.5,123.68,124.28,124.46,124.82,125,126,126.36,126.48,126.7,127.1,127.22,127.64,127.8,128.12,128.26,128.4,128.8]},{"s":5,"a":129.51,"b":135.41,"text":"Another is cramming everything the night before. Avoid it by spacing short sessions across days.","w":[129.6,130.12,130.3,130.84,131.28,131.42,131.62,132.64,133.02,133.14,133.36,133.84,134.12,134.58,134.96]},{"s":5,"a":135.71,"b":142.23,"text":"A third is practising only easy questions. Avoid it by mixing topics and using timed exam style questions.","w":[135.78,135.92,136.3,136.44,137.08,137.4,137.68,138.62,138.94,139.06,139.22,139.54,140.08,140.24,140.5,140.86,141.24,141.6]},{"s":6,"a":143.08,"b":148.28,"text":"Recap. Test yourself before you feel ready, and space your sessions instead of cramming.","w":[143.14,144.12,144.42,144.92,145.22,145.36,145.58,146.22,146.38,146.68,146.88,147.42,147.74,147.86]},{"s":6,"a":148.58,"b":151.95,"text":"Mix your topics, and explain ideas in your own words.","w":[148.66,148.86,149.02,149.86,149.98,150.52,150.92,151,151.24,151.42]},{"s":6,"a":152.25,"b":156.24,"text":"Practise under exam conditions, then mark and learn from mistakes.","w":[152.28,152.78,153.02,153.36,154.4,154.64,155.06,155.2,155.46,155.64]},{"s":6,"a":156.54,"b":161.26,"text":"Top tip: after each study session, write two questions you will answer tomorrow.","w":[156.56,156.86,157.64,157.98,158.22,158.54,159.18,159.48,159.76,160.26,160.36,160.58,160.86]},{"s":6,"a":161.56,"b":164.96,"text":"Knowzone is here to help you make every minute of revision count.","w":[161.64,162.26,162.4,162.62,162.74,162.92,163.06,163.48,163.78,164.08,164.18,164.6]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"The puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Confident with book closed; blank in exam"},{"at":5.65,"kind":"term","text":"Familiar ≠ known","detail":"The gap to solve: revise so it sticks"}]},{"at":14.06,"heading":"Why and how","items":[{"at":14.21,"kind":"term","text":"Dunlosky review","detail":"Rereading/highlighting help little; testing/spacing help most"},{"at":23.79,"kind":"term","text":"Recognition ≠ recall","detail":"Familiar feels like knowing"},{"at":30.1,"kind":"term","text":"Testing","detail":"Retrieval struggle strengthens memory"},{"at":35.67,"kind":"term","text":"Spacing","detail":"Leave a gap before returning"},{"at":35.67,"kind":"term","text":"Interleaving","detail":"Mix topics or question types"}]},{"at":43.1,"heading":"Five habits","items":[{"at":43.25,"kind":"text","text":"1 Test yourself before you feel ready"},{"at":51.16,"kind":"text","text":"2 Space it out: next day, 3–4 days, week"},{"at":58.01,"kind":"text","text":"3 Mix topics: mixed practice"},{"at":65.58,"kind":"text","text":"4 Explain in your own words"},{"at":71.71,"kind":"text","text":"5 Practise exam conditions; mark mistakes"}]},{"at":79.9,"heading":"Worked session: 25 min","items":[{"at":80.05,"kind":"text","text":"5 min: recall from memory, notes closed"},{"at":86.91,"kind":"text","text":"10 min: practice questions"},{"at":86.91,"kind":"text","text":"5 min: check mistakes + fix"},{"at":93.24,"kind":"text","text":"5 min: write questions for next time"}]},{"at":99.23,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":99.23,"kind":"question","text":"You have one hour for Biology tonight. Which is better: rereading the chapter twice, or closing the book and answering questions on it?"}]},{"at":112.78,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":112.93,"kind":"term","text":"Testing","detail":"Retrieves ideas and shows gaps"},{"at":112.93,"kind":"text","text":"Rereading only feels familiar"}]},{"at":121.74,"heading":"Common slips","items":[{"at":121.89,"kind":"wrong","text":"Rereading + highlighting = revision"},{"at":129.41,"kind":"wrong","text":"Cramming everything the night before"},{"at":135.61,"kind":"wrong","text":"Practising only easy questions"}]},{"at":142.83,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":142.98,"kind":"text","text":"Test before ready; space sessions"},{"at":148.48,"kind":"text","text":"Mix topics; explain in own words"},{"at":152.15,"kind":"text","text":"Practise exam conditions; mark mistakes"},{"at":156.44,"kind":"tip","text":"After each session, write two questions to answer tomorrow"}]}],"check":{"at":112.43,"seconds":10,"question":"You have one hour for Biology tonight. Which is better: rereading the chapter twice, or closing the book and answering questions on it?","answer":"Close the book and answer questions: testing retrieves ideas and shows gaps; rereading only feels familiar."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}